ion was moved and approved?","o":["Muhammad Ali Jinnah","Liaquat Ali Khan","Ayub Khan","Zulfikar Ali Bhutto"],"a":1},{"q":"How many principles of democracy, freedom, equality, tolerance, and social justice are mentioned in the features of the Objectives Resolution?","o":["3","5","7","10"],"a":3},{"q":"According to the Objectives Resolution, what will the state of Pakistan exercise its power through?","o":["Dictatorship","Representatives of the people","Monarchy","Military rule"],"a":1},{"q":"What concept does the Objectives Resolution emphasize regarding the sovereignty of God?","o":["Western democracy","Sovereignty lies in the people","Sovereignty of God","Dictatorship"],"a":2},{"q":"Why is the Objectives Resolution considered a framework in the constitutional history of Pakistan?","o":["It promotes a theocratic state","It is a basic document for constitutional development","It was abolished later","It is irrelevant to modern times"],"a":1}],"read":["Pakistan was born in August 1947, but a new country needs more than a border — it needs a soul, a statement of what it stands for. That statement came on **12 March 1949**, when Prime Minister **Liaquat Ali Khan** moved the **Objectives Resolution** in the first Constituent Assembly. Think of it as Pakistan writing down its founding beliefs before writing its actual constitution.","The first Constituent Assembly itself was created under the **Indian Independence Act 1947**, starting with **69 members**, and it wore two hats at once: making the constitution AND acting as the legislature (its dual function). The **All India Muslim League** dominated it, claiming to be the sole voice of Indian Muslims.","The Resolution's central idea was radical for a modern state: **sovereignty over the entire universe belongs to Almighty Allah**, and the people exercise authority as a sacred trust through their chosen representatives. It laid out principles of democracy, freedom, equality, tolerance and social justice. Non-Muslims objected — they feared this **mix of religion and politics** would empower religious extremists. Despite that, the Resolution endured: it later became the **Preamble** of every Pakistani constitution and the legal foundation of the ideology of Pakistan."],"box":[["Personalities","Liaquat Ali Khan (moved the Resolution; PM at the time)"],["Dates","12 March 1949 (Objectives Resolution approved)"],["Acts","Indian Independence Act 1947 (created the 1st Constituent Assembly)"],["Numbers","69 (initial members of 1st Constituent Assembly); 10 principles referenced"],["Organizations","All India Muslim League (dominant party)"],["Key concepts","Sovereignty of Allah; authority as a sacred trust; dual function = Constitution-making + Legislation"],["Outcomes","Became the Preamble of later constitutions; legal sanction to the Ideology of Pakistan"],["Examiner traps","Liaquat moved it (NOT Jinnah); date is 12 March 1949 (NOT 1947/48); sovereignty = Almighty Allah"]],"chain":["Pakistan created 1947","Need a founding ideology","Liaquat moves Objectives Resolution 12 Mar 1949","Sovereignty of Allah + rights as sacred trust","Becomes Preamble of future constitutions"],"sixty":["Objectives Resolution = 12 March 1949","Moved by Liaquat Ali Khan","1st Assembly = 69 members, under Indian Independence Act 1947","Sovereignty = Almighty Allah","Later became the Preamble"]},"17":{"title":"First Constituent Assembly & Basic Principles Committee","mcqs":[{"q":"What is a Constitution?","o":["A set of ordinary laws","A framework for governance and exercise of political power","A religious document","A historical artifact"],"a":1},{"q":"Which Act was modified and promulgated in the newly formed state of Pakistan?","o":["Government of India Act (1935)","Indian Independence Act (1947)","Mountbatten Plan Act","Simon Commission Act"],"a":0},{"q":"What were the major issues faced by the first Constituent Assembly?","o":["Federalism, Representation, and National Language","Democracy, Autonomy, and Shariah"