n East Pakistan?","o":["1947","1950","1952","1955"],"a":2},{"q":"Which constitutional system became dominant after the 1958 military takeover?","o":["Parliamentary System","Presidential System","Autocratic System","Monarchic System"],"a":1},{"q":"What was the major objection raised by East Pakistan against the First BPC Report?","o":["Lack of autonomy","Unequal representation","No mention of National Language","Oppression by West Pakistan"],"a":1},{"q":"What is the primary function of a constitution in a country?","o":["Create ordinary laws","Clarify the scope of power","Establish a theocratic state","Modify existing laws"],"a":1}],"read":["A **constitution** is not just a set of laws — it is the framework for governance and the exercise of political power, defining and clarifying the scope of authority in a country. When Pakistan formed, it had no constitution of its own, so it modified and promulgated the **Government of India Act 1935** as a stop-gap.","The first Constituent Assembly faced three big headaches: **Federalism, Representation, and the National Language**. To find a way forward it created the **Basic Principles Committee (BPC) on 12 March 1949** — the same day as the Objectives Resolution — to frame the basic principles for the future constitution.","Progress was painfully slow. The First BPC Report proposed representation based on population (which alarmed East Pakistan). Prime Minister **Muhammad Ali Bogra** offered the **Bogra Formula** to break the deadlock. Meanwhile the **One Unit scheme (October 1955)** merged West Pakistan, **Urdu** was adopted as national language (1954), the **Ulema Committee's 22 points** demanded the supremacy of the Qur'an and Sunnah, and the **Language Movement** flared in East Pakistan in **1952**. After the 1958 military takeover, the system shifted from parliamentary to **Presidential**."],"box":[["Committees","Basic Principles Committee (BPC), formed 12 March 1949; Ulema Committee (22 points)"],["Personalities","Muha